# The ONLINE page's third button (Options, proc 28: muProcOptWifi, which runs WiiConnect24 jobs)
# and With Anyone's Spectator (exit code 29) show only when nteGlobal says the service allows them
# (isEnableButton 0x8014FEDC, flags 0x20/0x40 and 0x10). Without a connection it never does; here
# the answer is always no, so neither button is drawn or can take the cursor whatever that data
# holds. muProcWifi's init (0x811799B8):
81179A04 38600020 38600020    # li r3, 0x20 (guard)
81179A08 4AFD64D5 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
81179A0C 28030001 28030001    # (guard)
81179A10 4082000C 4082000C    # (guard)
81179A14 38000001 38000001    # (guard)
81179A18 48000018 48000018    # (guard)
81179A1C 38600040 38600040    # li r3, 0x40 (guard)
81179A20 4AFD64BD 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
# muProcWifiAnybody's init (0x81191D44):
81191D7C 38600010 38600010    # li r3, 0x10 (guard)
81191D80 4AFBE15D 38600000    # bl isEnableButton -> li r3, 0
81191D84 7C7F1B78 7C7F1B78    # mr r31, r3 (guard)

# sqMenuMain::setNext's table (sora_scene, resident; one entry per main-menu exit code, which the
# menu leaves at [0x805A0060]+0x284): every online code goes to the local Versus character select
# that Group > Brawl (code 1) opens, through the block below. 24-27 are With Friends' modes
# (sqNetFriendList), 29 Spectator (sqNetAnyWatch), 30 With Anyone's Basic Brawl (sqNetAnyOkiraku),
# 31 its Team Battle (sqNetAnyTeamMelee): none of them is ever entered, so no online scene, no
# network thread, no DWC. The code itself stays as the menu wrote it: Orca reads it (30 Casual, 31
# Ranked, 24-27 Friends). 28 is a guard; 24-27 already point at the block's address (the friend
# list's entry, which the block replaces). Project+ keeps Brawl's sora_scene, so its file has the
# same groups.
80701A04 806DC8C8 806DC8C8    # code 24 (guard: the block below)
80701A08 806DC8C8 806DC8C8    # code 25 (guard)
80701A0C 806DC8C8 806DC8C8    # code 26 (guard)
80701A10 806DC8C8 806DC8C8    # code 27 (guard)
80701A14 806DC940 806DC940    # code 28 (guard)
80701A18 806DC8E0 806DC8C8    # code 29 (sqNetAnyWatch)
80701A1C 806DC8F8 806DC8C8    # code 30 (sqNetAnyOkiraku)
80701A20 806DC910 806DC8C8    # code 31 (sqNetAnyTeamMelee)

# Back out of that character select to where the player was, as the game does when it comes back
# from an online mode. sqVsMelee (sora_scene) leaves its character select for the menu in one place,
# setNext's state 16, always with setNextSequence("sqMenuMain", 1) (0x806DCE3C). sqMenuMain turns
# that argument into the menu's mode through its own switch (0x80701A4C: 0-22 stay, 23-26 become
# 24-27, 27-29 become 29-31, 30 and 31 become 34 and 35), muMenuMain::start (0x806CC038) reads the
# mode at [0x805A0060]+0x280, and the mode says where the menu opens: its page from a table
# (0x806F9228, a byte per mode) and the cursor on that page's button for it. Mode 1 is Group with
# Brawl selected; 29-31 With Anyone on Spectator, Casual (Basic Brawl) or Ranked (Team Battle);
# 34 and 35 the ONLINE page (proc 3) on With Friends or With Anyone (muProcWifi's init, 0x81179C2C,
# puts the cursor there for them); 24-27 were the With Friends page, which nothing opens any more
# (A on With Friends leaves the menu at once, above). So the block that starts sqVsMelee for an
# online code keeps that code in the sequence object, at +0x18 (sqVsMelee is 0x18 bytes in a
# 0x20-byte block; +0x18 to +0x1F keep the heap's fill through boots, menus and whole matches, in
# both games), and the back out takes it, once: an online code (24-31) goes back as its argument
# (With Friends' 24-27 as 30, the ONLINE page on With Friends; 29-31 -> 27-29), anything else (0,
# the heap's fill, a code already used) is 1 as before. Group > Brawl never sets it, so from there B
# still opens Group. A match in between keeps it: the results come back through sqPrizeCheck, which
# restarts sqVsMelee with 255 and leaves the object alone. Emulated code and memory only: both
# players run it, a rollback re-runs it, a drop-in keyframe carries the object.
#
# sqMenuMain::setNext's entries for codes 24-31 (0x806DC8C8 to 0x806DC924: the friend list's,
# Spectator's, Basic Brawl's and Team Battle's blocks, which the table above no longer reaches): the
# block, then the back out's helper. r18 is the scene manager, r19 the sequence names (0x807017B0,
# +48 "sqVsMelee"), r15 sqMenuMain, r30 its next state.
806DC8C8 80B20284 7E439378    # mr     r3, r18
806DC8CC 7E439378 38930030    # addi   r4, r19, 48 ("sqVsMelee", as Group > Brawl)
806DC8D0 38930160 38A00000    # li     r5, 0 (its argument, as Group > Brawl)
806DC8D4 4B950D6D 4B950D6D    # bl     setNextSequence (0x8002D640; guard)
806DC8D8 93CF0008 80720014    # lwz    r3, 0x14(r18) (the next sequence: sqVsMelee)
806DC8DC 480000F4 80120284    # lwz    r0, 0x284(r18) (the menu's exit code)
806DC8E0 7E439378 90030018    # stw    r0, 0x18(r3) (kept for the back out)
806DC8E4 38930170 93CF0008    # stw    r30, 0x8(r15)
806DC8E8 38A00000 480000E8    # b      0x806DC9D0 (setNext's loop, as every entry)
806DC8EC 4B950D55 80AF0018    # helper: lwz r5, 0x18(r15) (r15 is sqVsMelee there)
806DC8F0 93CF0008 38000000    # li     r0, 0
806DC8F4 480000DC 900F0018    # stw    r0, 0x18(r15) (used once)
806DC8F8 7E439378 3805FFE8    # subi   r0, r5, 24
806DC8FC 38930180 28000007    # cmplwi r0, 7
806DC900 38A00000 41810018    # bgt    0x806DC918 (not an online code)
806DC904 4B950D3D 38A5FFFE    # subi   r5, r5, 2
806DC908 93CF0008 28000004    # cmplwi r0, 4
806DC90C 480000C4 4C800020    # bgelr  (29-31: With Anyone, 27-29)
806DC910 7E439378 38A0001E    # li     r5, 30 (24-27: the ONLINE page on With Friends)
806DC914 38930190 4E800020    # blr
806DC918 38A00000 38A00001    # li     r5, 1 (Group, as before)
806DC91C 4B950D25 4E800020    # blr

# sqVsMelee::setNext's state 16 (0x806DCE34): the argument comes from the helper.
806DCE34 806F0014 806F0014    # lwz    r3, 0x14(r15) (guard)
806DCE38 38910064 38910064    # addi   r4, r17, 100 ("sqMenuMain"; guard)
806DCE3C 38A00001 4BFFFAB1    # li r5, 1 -> bl 0x806DC8EC (r5: the argument)
806DCE40 4B950801 4B950801    # bl     setNextSequence (guard)

# The online rules' lock on the way out (ORCA.md "Online rules"): while the match block's header
# (UX/MatchBlock.h, 0x806F2380) says casual or ranked, sqVsMelee::setNext's state 5, which takes the
# character select's result, sends anything but "go on" (its BACK button, held B) to state 4, the
# character select again, the way the stage select's B comes back to it (state 7), instead of
# state 16, the menu. No way out of the character select in a queue match; without a header
# (friends, solo) the menu as before, and so on the queue's own character select before a match
# (the header's FLAG_SOLO, UX/Queue.h), where B backing out ends the search. A toggled group
# (GamePatches.h): r24 is 4 and r20 16 through
# the whole function.
when 806F2380 == 59474D31               # the block's magic, 'YGM1'
when 806F2384 & 00FF0000 != 00000000    # its mode: casual or ranked
when 806F238C & 01000000 == 00000000    # not the queue's own character select (FLAG_SOLO): B backs out
806DCBFC 4182000C 4182000C    # beq    0x806DCC08 (go on: state 6, the stage select; guard)
806DCC00 928F0008 930F0008    # stw    r20, 8(r15) (16) -> stw r24, 8(r15) (4)
806DCC04 48000008 48000008    # b      0x806DCC0C (guard)
end

# --- Input: the game reads this frame's pads ------------------------------------------------------

# "Controller Input Lag Fix" [Magus], the line `C202AD8C 00000002 7C630214 3863FBFC` of the
# community "Global Brawl Netplay Settings" code in Dolphin's GameSettings/RSBE01.ini ("disables
# ... controller input lag"), which Project+ also ships in its codeset (so PPLUS32.patches doesn't
# carry it). The code at 0x8002AD88 copies one pad's 0x40-byte status out for the game (r4 the
# port, r5 the destination, r0 = port * 0x40) from the array at r3 + 0x444; the Gecko code inserts
# `subi r3, r3, 0x404` after its `add`, so it copies from the array at r3 + 0x40, which is a frame
# newer: vanilla Brawl answers a press a frame sooner, as Project+ does (its first changed image is
# the copy of press+3 instead of press+4). Here the same addresses come from the copy's own
# displacements (0x444.. -> 0x40..), in place, with no code cave: nothing else reads r3 before
# `li r3, 1; blr`. The stores between the loads are guard words, so the group is one run of 45 and
# lands once, at boot, whole. Emulated code: both players run it (the file is in the lobby key), and
# a rollback or a drop-in keyframe carries it in RAM.
8002AD8C 7C630214 7C630214    # add   r3, r3, r0 (guard only)
8002AD90 80030444 80030040    # lwz   r0, 0x444(r3) -> 0x40(r3)
8002AD94 90050000 90050000    # stw   r0, 0x0(r5) (guard only)
8002AD98 80030448 80030044    # lwz   r0, 0x448(r3) -> 0x44(r3)
8002AD9C 90050004 90050004    # stw   r0, 0x4(r5) (guard only)
8002ADA0 8003044C 80030048    # lwz   r0, 0x44C(r3) -> 0x48(r3)
8002ADA4 90050008 90050008    # stw   r0, 0x8(r5) (guard only)
8002ADA8 80030450 8003004C    # lwz   r0, 0x450(r3) -> 0x4C(r3)
8002ADAC 9005000C 9005000C    # stw   r0, 0xC(r5) (guard only)
8002ADB0 80030454 80030050    # lwz   r0, 0x454(r3) -> 0x50(r3)
8002ADB4 90050010 90050010    # stw   r0, 0x10(r5) (guard only)
8002ADB8 80030458 80030054    # lwz   r0, 0x458(r3) -> 0x54(r3)
8002ADBC 90050014 90050014    # stw   r0, 0x14(r5) (guard only)
8002ADC0 C003045C C0030058    # lfs   f0, 0x45C(r3) -> 0x58(r3)
8002ADC4 D0050018 D0050018    # stfs  f0, 0x18(r5) (guard only)
8002ADC8 C0030460 C003005C    # lfs   f0, 0x460(r3) -> 0x5C(r3)
8002ADCC D005001C D005001C    # stfs  f0, 0x1C(r5) (guard only)
8002ADD0 C0030464 C0030060    # lfs   f0, 0x464(r3) -> 0x60(r3)
8002ADD4 D0050020 D0050020    # stfs  f0, 0x20(r5) (guard only)
8002ADD8 C0030468 C0030064    # lfs   f0, 0x468(r3) -> 0x64(r3)
8002ADDC D0050024 D0050024    # stfs  f0, 0x24(r5) (guard only)
8002ADE0 C003046C C0030068    # lfs   f0, 0x46C(r3) -> 0x68(r3)
8002ADE4 D0050028 D0050028    # stfs  f0, 0x28(r5) (guard only)
8002ADE8 C0030470 C003006C    # lfs   f0, 0x470(r3) -> 0x6C(r3)
8002ADEC D005002C D005002C    # stfs  f0, 0x2C(r5) (guard only)
8002ADF0 88030474 88030070    # lbz   r0, 0x474(r3) -> 0x70(r3)
8002ADF4 98050030 98050030    # stb   r0, 0x30(r5) (guard only)
8002ADF8 88030475 88030071    # lbz   r0, 0x475(r3) -> 0x71(r3)
8002ADFC 98050031 98050031    # stb   r0, 0x31(r5) (guard only)
8002AE00 88030476 88030072    # lbz   r0, 0x476(r3) -> 0x72(r3)
8002AE04 98050032 98050032    # stb   r0, 0x32(r5) (guard only)
8002AE08 88030477 88030073    # lbz   r0, 0x477(r3) -> 0x73(r3)
8002AE0C 98050033 98050033    # stb   r0, 0x33(r5) (guard only)
8002AE10 88030478 88030074    # lbz   r0, 0x478(r3) -> 0x74(r3)
8002AE14 98050034 98050034    # stb   r0, 0x34(r5) (guard only)
8002AE18 88030479 88030075    # lbz   r0, 0x479(r3) -> 0x75(r3)
8002AE1C 98050035 98050035    # stb   r0, 0x35(r5) (guard only)
8002AE20 8803047A 88030076    # lbz   r0, 0x47A(r3) -> 0x76(r3)
8002AE24 98050036 98050036    # stb   r0, 0x36(r5) (guard only)
8002AE28 8803047B 88030077    # lbz   r0, 0x47B(r3) -> 0x77(r3)
8002AE2C 98050037 98050037    # stb   r0, 0x37(r5) (guard only)
8002AE30 8803047C 88030078    # lbz   r0, 0x47C(r3) -> 0x78(r3)
8002AE34 98050038 98050038    # stb   r0, 0x38(r5) (guard only)
8002AE38 80030480 8003007C    # lwz   r0, 0x480(r3) -> 0x7C(r3)
8002AE3C 9005003C 9005003C    # stw   r0, 0x3C(r5) (guard only)

# Project+'s "Move v-sync call for Brawl/PM" [hannesmann, Dan Salvato, Achilles], the same two words
# (Source/Extras/Netplay.asm on Project+'s SD card). Brawl's frame function (0x80023AE4) waits for a
# retrace before it draws: the beq at 0x80023B88 goes back to VIWaitForRetrace (0x801E892C) while no
# XFB slot is free. Moved, it never waits there and waits once at the end instead, so each frame is
# drawn and copied right after the game's logic, about 13 ms sooner (ORCA.md "Input latency"). Both
# groups are in the main DOL, so they land together at the first boundary and stay.
80023B84 2C000000 2C000000    # cmpwi r0, 0 (guard)
80023B88 4182FF7C 60000000    # beq   0x80023B04 -> nop
80023B8C 881F0074 881F0074    # lbz   r0, 0x74(r31) (guard)

80024020 7C0803A6 7C0803A6    # mtlr  r0 (guard)
80024024 38210020 38210020    # addi  r1, r1, 0x20 (guard)
80024028 4E800020 481C4904    # blr -> b 0x801E892C (VIWaitForRetrace, a tail call)

# --- Everything unlocked --------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The prize-unlock check (0x8004F978) reads any bit outside its 102-prize table as set for bits
# 0-11 of each word (the community code's choice).
8004FAE4 7C03002E 38000FFF    # lwzx r0, r3, r0 -> li r0, 0xFFF (the prize-unlock check)
8004E7D0 540307FE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x01 -> always 1 (Solo: All-Star)
8004E7F4 5403FFFE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x02 -> always 1 (Stadium: Boss Battles)
8004E818 5403F7FE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x04 -> always 1 (community code)
8004E83C 5403EFFE 38600001    # record +0x268 bit 0x08 -> always 1 (community code)

# sqPrizeCheck (sora_scene, 0x806F5B64, state at +0x8): at boot it walks the prizes that are
# unlocked and not yet announced (the list at 0x806F5FB8: four tables, the last ones derived, such
# as co-op events from the roster) and, if any, shows them on scPrizeInfo before the menu. The
# list is still built (it marks what it finds as announced, as the game does), but state 10 always
# goes on to state 14, the menu, never to the announcement. The first table is skipped outright so
# its entries can never fill the 16-entry list before the derived tables have run.
806F603C 40820084 48000084    # bne- +0x84 -> b +0x84 (first table: skip the prize)
806F5EB0 40820010 48000010    # bne- +0x10 -> b +0x10 (state 10: never scPrizeInfo)

