# --- Rules: items off by default ------------------------------------------------------------------

# gmGlobalRecord (0x9017B640) keeps the rules menu's item settings at +0x810, the record's menu data
# (GameGlobal::getGlobalRecordMenuDatap, 0x8004E5CC): +0 the item frequency (one byte: 0 None, 1
# Low, 2 Medium, 3 High) and +8 the item switch (64 bits, one per item). GameGlobal::init
# (0x8004C63C, its stores at 0x8004C9CC-0x8004C9D8) sets Medium with every item on before the
# first frame, so before any patch here can apply, and a session has no save to replace them: every
# match had items. Rules > Item Switch (muProcItemSwitch, in the resident rules module at
# 0x806A3A60: it reads these words at 0x806AA62C and writes them back at 0x806AA9B8) edits them in
# place, and a match takes its items from there. Instead, once: the frequency None, so no item
# appears. The switch is left as the game set it, so choosing a frequency there brings the usual
# items back. The group is written only while all four words hold the game's defaults; its second
# word (menu data +4, padding after the frequency byte: no load or store of record +0x811-0x817 in
# the DOL or the boot, menu and match modules) becomes 'ORCA', so it never lands again and a player
# who picks Medium with every item on keeps it (without the marker that choice went back to None
# in the same frame). All of it is emulated memory: a friend who drops in from the host's keyframe
# gets the host's settings and marker, and a re-run sees what the first run saw. Found with
# ORCA_UX_PROBE watches on the record and on the Item Switch page (its frequency at +0x678) under
# scripted visits; the community "Global Brawl Netplay Settings" code in Dolphin's
# GameSettings/RSBE01.ini writes the same words (and the stage list and the stock and time rules,
# not changed here).
9017BE50 02000000 00000000    # item frequency: Medium -> None
9017BE54 00000000 4F524341    # menu data +4 (unused): 'ORCA', the group has landed
9017BE58 FFFFFFFF FFFFFFFF    # item switch: every item on (guard only, left as is)
9017BE5C FFFFFFFF FFFFFFFF    # (guard only)

# --- Options > Sound: the balance stays in the middle ---------------------------------------------

# Options > Sound has one slider, the balance between music and sound effects: gmGlobalRecord menu
# data +0x18 (0x9017BE68), a byte from 0 to 100. GameGlobal::init (0x8004C7E0) sets 50, where both
# play in full, before the first frame, and a session has no save to change it. The page
# (sora_menu_main; its input handler at 0x8117B2CC, the value it edits at +0x66C) moves the value
# one step a frame while a direction is held and hands each step straight to the sound manager
# (0x80076F30 on [0x805A01D0]): toward 100, the end labelled MUSIC, the music fades out; toward 0,
# the effects. Leaving the page stores the value in the record. All of that is game memory, so in a
# room it was the host's: a joiner plays on a copy of the host's machine, and a host who had pushed
# the slider to an end silenced the friend's music or effects (two Orcas, 2026-10-07). The player's
# own Music switch (ORCA.md "Music switch") changes only what their machine plays.
#
# Instead both of the slider's limits are 50, so it never moves, in every session: an Orca that
# plays alone is still the host a friend can drop into, and a balance set alone would reach the
# friend with the keyframe. The value never changes, so the sound manager is never called and
# leaving the page stores what was there. Menu code only, run while the page is open: nothing per
# frame, nothing in a match. Left's limit is `cmplwi r0, 100; bge` (stop at or over it); right's
# was `cmpwi r0, 0; beq`, now `ble`, so a value under the limit only ever comes up to it (never
# below 0 into the u16's top). The words checked in RAM on the Sound page, Brawl and Project+,
# 2026-10-07. The game's only other calls to 0x80076F30 are its init, its reset to the defaults and
# a save's load (0x8004C7F0, 0x8004E294, 0x8004ECB8), and Project+'s codeset makes none.
# To clamp instead of lock (10 to 90, so some balance stays but no end mutes anyone): 2800005A for
# left's limit and 2C00000A for right's, in both files (the Project+ lines are these + 0x1480).
8117B43C A003066C A003066C    # lhz    r0, 0x66C(r3) (the slider's value; guard)
8117B440 28000064 28000032    # cmplwi r0, 100 -> 50: left stops at 50
8117B444 408000B4 408000B4    # bge    0x8117B4F8 (to the right check; guard)

8117B500 A003066C A003066C    # lhz    r0, 0x66C(r3) (guard)
8117B504 2C000000 2C000032    # cmpwi  r0, 0 -> 50: right stops at 50
8117B508 418200B0 408100B0    # beq -> ble 0x8117B5B8 (the handler's end)

# --- Fights: no random tripping -------------------------------------------------------------------

# "No Tripping" [Phantom Wings], the line `0489E910 FFE0F090` of the community "Global Brawl Netplay
# Settings" code in Dolphin's GameSettings/RSBE01.ini ("disables tripping"), the competitive and
# netplay standard. A session runs no Gecko codes (Profile.cpp: MAIN_ENABLE_CHEATS off), so through
# 0.3.39 Brawl in Orca tripped at random. Project+ has no tripping of its own, so PPLUS32.patches
# doesn't carry it. The trip check (0x8089E820, in sora_melee, which stays loaded from boot) fetches
# the trip rate param (0xDB3) into f31 and returns true when rate * (chance + f30) beats a random
# number (0x8003FB64), and false without drawing one when that product is 0. The Gecko word makes
# the rate f30, the call's own float argument, which is 0: with it the fight never draws there (a
# harness run of the default inputs: g_mtRandDefault parts at the first check, scMelee frame 402)
# and nobody trips. The same word, guarded by the param fetch around it; it lands once, at boot.
# Emulated code: both players run it (the file is in the lobby key), and a rollback or a drop-in
# keyframe carries it in RAM. Read in RAM on scMelee, 2026-10-11.
8089E900 7FA3EB78 7FA3EB78    # mr     r3, r29 (the fighter; guard)
8089E904 38800DB3 38800DB3    # li     r4, 0xDB3 (the trip rate param; guard)
8089E908 38A00000 38A00000    # li     r5, 0 (guard)
8089E90C 4BEF8361 4BEF8361    # bl     0x80796C6C (the param's float; guard)
8089E910 FFE00890 FFE0F090    # fmr    f31, f1 -> fmr f31, f30: the rate is the argument, 0
8089E914 2C1E0001 2C1E0001    # cmpwi  r30, 1 (the trip's kind; guard)
